// Koru Standard Library: the `owning` part of string.kz.
// Joined via `~part owning` in string.kz; merged into the module at load.

// The two ownership hand-offs — lifted for the line wall. `snapshot`
// copies out (the String keeps living); `detach` moves out (the String
// ends and the slice's ownership transfers to the caller).

// ============================================
// Snapshot - the escape path (610_007, ruled 2026-09-05)
// ============================================
//
// `read` (`{ s } -> string` via `-> s.data`) retired here: a returned borrow
// is unpoliceable — the owner can invalidate it by discharge OR mutation —
// so borrows flow down, never up (KORU095 refuses the projection). Consumers
// that used `read` to feed a call take the handle instead (borrow-for-the-
// call, `{{ h:S }}`); genuine escapes copy out through this tor. The copy
// carries `<view!>` so it cannot leak the way a bare allocating `string`
// return would. One memcpy, at a boundary, priced as noise.
~pub tor snapshot { s: *String<view|instance> }
| ok *String<view!>
| err string
~snapshot = std/string:from-page(text: s.data)
| ok s2 => ok s2
| err e => err e

// The move-sibling of `snapshot`: consume the String and yield its bytes
// as an owned `string` — no copy, no borrow. `snapshot` copies out and the
// String keeps living; `detach` ends the String and hands the slice's
// ownership to the caller, for custody hand-offs like a Response body
// whose `body_allocated` frees it after send. It returns a bare `string`
// on purpose: the contract is "an owned slice, now yours to dispose" —
// tracked no further because the next holder owns it outright.
~pub tor detach { s: *String<!view|!instance> } -> string
~proc detach|zig {
    const out = s.data;
    s.allocator.destroy(s);
    return out;
}
